/*!
    \page index.html
    \title Chatbot

    \brief An OpenAI compatible LLM chatbot written with QML and Felgo.

    This demo shows how to build a chatgpt like AI chatbot that connects with any OpenAI
    compatible API and can run on any platform using Felgo and Qt technologies.

    The tutorial follows the same order in which the app was built: we start with an empty
    project, create the UI, store the API configuration, connect to the API and finally add
    the finishing touches like a typing indicator and persistent chat history. You can follow
    along step by step, the complete source is also available in this repository.

    \section1 Introduction

    The finished app talks to any \l{OpenAI Chat API Reference}{OpenAI compatible API}. That
    includes the OpenAI platform itself, but also local servers like LM Studio, Ollama or
    llama.cpp, which all expose the same \c /v1/chat/completions and \c /v1/models endpoints.
    This makes the app a nice client for locally running models.

    The app consists of five QML files, each with a single responsibility:

    \list
        \li \c Main.qml - the app shell, the chat logic and the chat persistence.
        \li \c ChatApi.qml - everything related to the HTTP calls, without any UI.
        \li \c ChatView.qml - a reusable list of chat bubbles.
        \li \c InputBar.qml - the message input row with the send button.
        \li \c SettingsDialog.qml - the API configuration dialog with a model dropdown.
    \endlist

    Splitting the app this way keeps each file easy to read: the UI components
    (\c ChatView, \c InputBar, \c SettingsDialog) do not know anything about the API, and
    \c ChatApi does not know anything about the UI. \c Main.qml connects the pieces.

    \section1 Creating the project

    Download the \l{Install Felgo}{felgo} SDK and create a new Single Page Application from the
    new project dialog.

    \image {screenshot-new-project.png}{The Felgo new project dialog with "Single Page Application" selected.}

    This will create a simple demo project with everything needed to build the app for required
    platforms up and running.

    \section1 The app shell: Main.qml

    The \l{App} type is the root item of every Felgo app. Inside it we use a
    \c NavigationStack with a single \c AppPage, which gives us a header with a title and
    space for action buttons.

    We add two actions to the header: a cog icon that opens the settings dialog and a trash
    icon that clears the conversation. Below the page we place a \c ChatView (covered in the
    next section) and the \c InputBar with the text field and the send button, anchored to
    the bottom of the screen.

    \code
    NavigationStack {

        AppPage {
            title: qsTr("Chatbot")

            rightBarItem: IconButtonBarItem {
                iconType: IconType.cog
                title: "Settings"

                onClicked: settingsDialog.open()
            }

            leftBarItem: IconButtonBarItem {
                iconType: IconType.trash
                title: "Clear chat"

                onClicked: clearDialog.open()
            }

            SettingsDialog {
                id: settingsDialog
                settingsStore: app.settings
            }

            ChatView {
                id: chatView
                anchors {
                    top: parent.top
                    left: parent.left
                    right: parent.right
                    bottom: messageInput.top
                    margins: dp(20)
                }

                messages: chatMessages
            }

            InputBar {
                id: messageInput

                anchors {
                    left: parent.left
                    right: parent.right
                    bottom: parent.bottom
                    margins: dp(20)
                }

                busy: app.busy

                onSendRequested: message => sendMessage(message)
            }
        }

    }
    \endcode

    The \c InputBar only collects the user input and reports it with its \c sendRequested
    signal. The \c busy property disables the input while the app waits for an API response,
    so the user cannot send a second message while the first one is still pending. The
    component itself looks like this:

    \code
    Row {
        id: root

        property bool busy: false

        signal sendRequested(string message)

        spacing: dp(8)
        height: dp(45)

        function submit() {
            sendRequested(inputMessage.text.trim())
            inputMessage.text = ""
        }

        AppTextField {
            id: inputMessage
            placeholderText: "Send a message"
            width: root.width - sendButton.width - root.spacing
            height: dp(45)
            enabled: !root.busy

            onAccepted: root.submit()
        }

        AppButton {
            id: sendButton

            width: dp(100)
            height: dp(45)
            text: root.busy ? "..." : "➡️"
            enabled: !root.busy

            onClicked: root.submit()
        }
    }
    \endcode

    \image {screenshot-app-shell.png}{The empty app with the header bar and the input row.}

    \section1 Displaying the messages: ChatView.qml

    The chat itself lives in \c ChatView.qml, a plain \c Item that receives the message model
    as a required property. Inside it, an \l{AppListView} renders every entry of the model as
    a chat bubble.

    Each message is a \c ListModel entry with a \c role and a \c content field. The roles are:

    \list
        \li \c system - the instruction for the model, never rendered.
        \li \c user - a message from the user, blue bubble on the right.
        \li \c assistant - an answer from the model, green bubble on the left.
        \li \c typing - internal placeholder shown while waiting for the API.
        \li \c error - a failed request, red bubble on the left.
    \endlist

    \code
    delegate: Item {
        id: messageDelegate

        required property string role
        required property string content

        width: chatView.width

        height: role === "user" || role === "assistant" || role === "error" ? bubble.height
              : role === "typing" ? typingBubble.height
              : 0

        Rectangle {
            id: bubble
            visible: messageDelegate.role === "user"
                     || messageDelegate.role === "assistant"
                     || messageDelegate.role === "error"

            property bool isMe: messageDelegate.role === "user"

            width: Math.min(
                       messageText.implicitWidth + dp(24),
                       messageDelegate.width * 0.75)
            height: messageText.implicitHeight + dp(20)

            radius: dp(14)
            color: isMe ? "#2878f0"
                  : messageDelegate.role === "error" ? "#c62828"
                  : "#1f8b03"

            anchors.right: isMe ? parent.right : undefined
            anchors.left: isMe ? undefined : parent.left

            AppText {
                id: messageText

                anchors.fill: parent
                anchors.margins: dp(12)

                text: messageDelegate.content
                color: "white"

                wrapMode: Text.Wrap
                font.pixelSize: sp(15)
            }
        }
        // ... the typing indicator bubble follows here, see the section below
    }
    \endcode

    The system message gets a delegate height of \c 0, so it exists in the model for the API
    but never appears in the UI. Error bubbles are excluded from the conversation that we send
    back to the API, which is covered in the section about the request.

    \image {screenshot-chat-bubbles.png}{A short conversation with one user message, one assistant answer and one red error bubble}

    \section1 Remembering the configuration: SettingsDialog.qml

    The app needs three pieces of configuration: the API base URL, the API key and the model
    name. We store them with the \l{Storage} component, which Felgo provides on every
    \l{App} as \c app.settings. Values written there survive app restarts.

    The dialog exposes its storage keys as readonly properties:

    \code
    readonly property string apiUrl: "apiUrl"
    readonly property string apiKey: "apiKey"
    readonly property string apiModel: "apiModel"
    \endcode

    This way \c Main.qml can read and write the same values without duplicating the key
    strings: \c app.settings.getValue(settingsDialog.apiUrl).

    The dialog loads the stored values every time it opens and writes them back when the user
    presses Save. Invalid input (empty fields) is rejected with a red error text below the
    fields.

    \image {screenshot-settings-dialog.png}{The settings dialog with all fields filled}

    \section1 Choosing a model: the model dropdown

    Instead of typing a model name manually, the settings dialog can fetch the list of
    available models from the server. Every OpenAI compatible API exposes a \c /models
    endpoint which answers with a list like this:

    \code
    {
      "object": "list",
      "data": [
        { "id": "gpt-4o", "object": "model", ... },
        { "id": "gpt-4o-mini", "object": "model", ... }
      ]
    }
    \endcode

    A press on the \c Fetch button next to the dropdown sends a \c GET request to \c <baseURL>/models
    with the same authorization header as the chat requests:

    \code
    HttpRequest
        .get(url + "/models")
        .timeout(10000)
        .set("Authorization", "Bearer " + key)
        .then(res => {
            // extract res.body.data[].id
        }).catch(err => {
            // show error text
        })
    \endcode

    The fetched ids fill a standard \l{ComboBox} from Qt Quick Controls with
    \c editable set to \c true. The editable property is important: if the fetch fails or the
    server does not implement \c /models, the user can still type a model name manually and
    the app keeps working.

    Fetching the model list doubles as a connection test: if the request succeeds, we know
    that the URL and the key are valid.

    The fetched list is also cached in the settings store, so the dropdown is filled again
    after the next app start without a new request.

    \section1 Talking to the API: ChatApi.qml

    The HTTP calls live in their own component, \c ChatApi.qml, which contains no UI at all.
    Keeping them separate from \c Main.qml makes both files easier to read and lets you swap
    the API part without touching the interface.

    Sending a message is a single POST request to \c <baseURL>/chat/completions. The base URL
    usually ends with \c /v1, for example \c https://api.openai.com/v1 or
    \c http://localhost:1234/v1 for a local LM Studio server. The app simply appends the
    endpoint path to whatever URL is configured, so the \c /v1 part is part of the base URL.

    We use Felgo's \l{HttpRequest} type, a promise based REST client. The component offers a
    single \c request() function that reports the result with an \c onSuccess or \c onError
    callback:

    \code
    function request(url, apiKey, model, messages, onSuccess, onError) {
        const chatUrl = url.replace(/\/+$/, "") + "/chat/completions"

        HttpRequest
            .post(chatUrl)
            .timeout(30000)
            .set("Content-Type", "application/json")
            .set("Authorization", `Bearer ${apiKey}`)
            .send({
                      model: model,
                      messages: messages,
                      temperature: 0.7
                  })
            .then(res => {
                // validate the answer and call onSuccess
            }).catch(err => onError(requestErrorMessage(err)))
    }
    \endcode

    The \c messages array is the complete conversation, built from the \c ListModel with
    \c listModelToArray(). This function filters the model entries: only the roles the API
    actually understands (\c system, \c user, \c assistant) are sent, and each entry is
    reduced to exactly the two fields \c role and \c content. Without this filter, internal
    entries like the typing indicator or the error bubbles would end up in the request and
    the API would reject it with a validation error.

    \code
    function listModelToArray(model) {
        const allowedRoles = [ "system", "user", "assistant" ]

        let result = []

        for (let i = 0; i < model.count; i++) {
            const item = model.get(i)

            if (allowedRoles.indexOf(item.role) !== -1)
                result.push({ role: item.role, content: item.content })
        }

        return result
    }
    \endcode

    The system prompt from the beginning of the conversation is part of every request, so the
    model keeps its instruction for the whole chat.

    In \c Main.qml, sending a message is then reduced to appending the user message, showing
    the typing indicator and handing everything over to the API component:

    \code
    chatApi.request(apiUrl, apiKey, apiModel,
                    chatApi.listModelToArray(chatMessages),
                    function(answer) {
                        removeTypingIndicator()
                        app.busy = false
                        chatMessages.append({ role: "assistant", content: answer })
                        chatView.scrollToBottom()
                        saveHistory()
                    },
                    function(error) {
                        removeTypingIndicator()
                        app.busy = false
                        chatMessages.append({ role: "error", content: error })
                        chatView.scrollToBottom()
                    })
    \endcode

    \section1 The typing indicator and error handling

    While the request is pending, the model is thinking and the user sees a placeholder
    bubble with three blinking dots. The placeholder is a normal model entry with the
    internal role \c typing:

    \code
    chatMessages.append({ role: "typing", content: "" })
    \endcode

    The dots are three small \c Rectangle items in a \c Row, animated with a staggered
    \c SequentialAnimation on their opacity:

    \code
    Repeater {
        model: 3

        Rectangle {
            width: dp(8)
            height: dp(8)
            radius: dp(4)
            color: "white"
            opacity: 0.3

            SequentialAnimation on opacity {
                loops: Animation.Infinite

                PauseAnimation { duration: index * 200 }
                NumberAnimation { from: 0.3; to: 1.0; duration: 250 }
                NumberAnimation { from: 1.0; to: 0.3; duration: 250 }
                PauseAnimation { duration: 700 }
            }
        }
    }
    \endcode

    As soon as the response arrives, the placeholder is removed and replaced with the whole
    message at once. Since the input is disabled while a request is pending, the typing
    bubble is always the last entry of the model, which makes removing it trivial:

    \code
    function removeTypingIndicator() {
        const last = chatMessages.get(chatMessages.count - 1)

        if (last && last.role === "typing")
            chatMessages.remove(chatMessages.count - 1)
    }
    \endcode

    Errors appear as red bubbles in the chat, so the user always sees what went wrong. The
    error message is built from the request error: if the server sent a description (for
    example for an invalid API key), we show it, otherwise we fall back to a generic message.
    The \c requestErrorMessage() function also lives in \c ChatApi.qml, next to the request
    it describes.

    \code
    function requestErrorMessage(err) {
        const body = err && err.response ? err.response.body : null

        if (body && body.error && body.error.message)
            return body.error.message

        if (err && err.status === 0)
            return "Could not reach the server. Check the API URL and your connection."

        return err && err.message ? err.message : "Request failed. Please try again."
    }
    \endcode

    In addition, the response itself is validated before it is used: if the answer does not
    contain \c choices[0].message, the user gets an "unexpected response" error instead of a
    JavaScript exception.

    \image {screenshot-typing-indicator.png}{The typing indicator bubble visible in the chat while a request is pending}

    \section1 Keeping the conversation

    The chat history is stored in the settings store as a JSON string, so the conversation
    survives an app restart. Saving happens after every new message:

    \code
    function saveHistory() {
        let history = []

        for (let i = 0; i < chatMessages.count; i++) {
            const item = chatMessages.get(i)

            if (item.role === "system" || item.role === "user" || item.role === "assistant")
                history.push({ role: item.role, content: item.content })
        }

        app.settings.setValue("chatHistory", JSON.stringify(history))
    }
    \endcode

    On startup, the system prompt is added first, then the saved conversation is parsed and
    appended after it. A corrupted history file simply results in a fresh chat, it never
    crashes the app.

    The trash icon in the header opens a confirmation dialog and clears both the model and
    the stored history. Note that the system prompt is re-added immediately, so the app is
    ready for a new conversation right away.

    \section1 Ideas to extend the app

    The demo intentionally keeps things small. If you want to take it further, here are some
    natural next steps:

    \list
        \li Multiple named conversations, stored as separate history entries.
        \li Trimming the sent history to the last N messages to stay within the context window.
        \li A context menu on chat bubbles to copy or delete single messages.
        \li Rendering markdown in assistant messages.
    \endlist

    This demo contains no secrets in its code: the API key is entered by the user and lives
    only on their device in the settings store. Never hardcode an API key in the QML sources.
*/
